## Part 3: JavaScript - Bringing It to Life

### What is JavaScript?

JavaScript is a **programming language** that adds interactivity and dynamic behavior to websites. It's the only programming language that runs natively in web browsers.

**What can JavaScript do?**
- Respond to user actions (clicks, typing, scrolling)
- Modify HTML and CSS dynamically
- Fetch data from servers
- Create animations and effects
- Validate forms
- Store data locally
- And much, much more!

### Adding JavaScript to Your Page

```html
<!-- External JS file (Best Practice!) -->
<script src="script.js"></script>

<!-- Internal JS -->
<script>
    console.log('Hello from JavaScript!');
</script>

<!-- Add at end of body for best performance -->
<body>
    <!-- Your HTML -->
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
```

### JavaScript Fundamentals

#### 1. Variables and Data Types

```javascript
// Variables - containers for data
let name = "John";           // Can be reassigned
const age = 30;              // Cannot be reassigned (use for constants)
var oldWay = "Avoid this";   // Old way, use let/const instead

// Data Types
let string = "Hello";        // Text
let number = 42;             // Numbers
let boolean = true;          // true or false
let array = [1, 2, 3];       // List of values
let object = {               // Key-value pairs
    firstName: "John",
    lastName: "Doe",
    age: 30
};
let nothing = null;          // Intentionally empty
let notDefined = undefined;  // Not yet defined

// Accessing data
console.log(name);           // Output: John
console.log(array[0]);       // Output: 1 (arrays are 0-indexed)
console.log(object.firstName); // Output: John
```

#### 2. Operators

```javascript
// Arithmetic
let sum = 5 + 3;             // 8
let difference = 10 - 4;     // 6
let product = 4 * 5;         // 20
let quotient = 20 / 4;       // 5
let remainder = 10 % 3;      // 1
let power = 2 ** 3;          // 8 (2 to the power of 3)

// Comparison (returns true or false)
5 === 5                      // true (equal value and type)
5 == "5"                     // true (equal value, ignores type)
5 !== 3                      // true (not equal)
5 > 3                        // true
5 < 3                        // false
5 >= 5                       // true

// Logical
true && false                // AND: false
true || false                // OR: true
!true                        // NOT: false

// String operations
let greeting = "Hello" + " " + "World";  // "Hello World"
let template = `Hello ${name}`;          // Template literal: "Hello John"
```

#### 3. Functions

```javascript
// Function declaration
function greet(name) {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

console.log(greet("Alice"));  // Output: Hello, Alice!

// Arrow function (modern, shorter syntax)
const greetArrow = (name) => {
    return `Hello, ${name}!`;
};

// Even shorter for single expression
const greetShort = (name) => `Hello, ${name}!`;

// Function with default parameter
function multiply(a, b = 1) {
    return a * b;
}

console.log(multiply(5));     // 5 (uses default b = 1)
console.log(multiply(5, 3));  // 15
```

#### 4. Conditionals

```javascript
// If-else
let age = 18;

if (age >= 18) {
    console.log("Adult");
} else if (age >= 13) {
    console.log("Teenager");
} else {
    console.log("Child");
}

// Ternary operator (shorthand)
let status = age >= 18 ? "Adult" : "Minor";

// Switch statement
let day = "Monday";

switch (day) {
    case "Monday":
        console.log("Start of week");
        break;
    case "Friday":
        console.log("Almost weekend!");
        break;
    default:
        console.log("Regular day");
}
```

#### 5. Loops

```javascript
// For loop
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    console.log(i);  // 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
}

// While loop
let count = 0;
while (count < 5) {
    console.log(count);
    count++;
}

// For...of loop (iterate over arrays)
let fruits = ["apple", "banana", "orange"];
for (let fruit of fruits) {
    console.log(fruit);
}

// Array methods (modern approach)
fruits.forEach((fruit) => {
    console.log(fruit);
});
```

### DOM Manipulation - The Key to Interactivity!

The **DOM** (Document Object Model) is how JavaScript interacts with HTML.

#### Selecting Elements

```javascript
// Get single element
const element = document.getElementById('myId');
const element = document.querySelector('.myClass');      // First match
const element = document.querySelector('#myId');         // By ID

// Get multiple elements
const elements = document.querySelectorAll('.myClass');  // Returns NodeList
const elements = document.getElementsByClassName('myClass'); // Returns HTMLCollection
```

#### Modifying Content and Styles

```javascript
// Change text content
element.textContent = "New text";
element.innerHTML = "<strong>Bold text</strong>";

// Change styles
element.style.color = "red";
element.style.backgroundColor = "yellow";
element.style.fontSize = "20px";

// Add/remove classes
element.classList.add('active');
element.classList.remove('hidden');
element.classList.toggle('visible');  // Add if not present, remove if present

// Change attributes
element.setAttribute('src', 'new-image.jpg');
element.src = 'new-image.jpg';  // Direct property
const value = element.getAttribute('data-id');
```

#### Creating and Removing Elements

```javascript
// Create new element
const newDiv = document.createElement('div');
newDiv.textContent = 'Hello!';
newDiv.classList.add('box');

// Add to page
document.body.appendChild(newDiv);           // Add to end
parentElement.insertBefore(newDiv, existingElement);

// Remove element
element.remove();
parentElement.removeChild(childElement);
```

### Event Handling - Responding to User Actions

```javascript
// Click event
const button = document.querySelector('#myButton');

button.addEventListener('click', function() {
    alert('Button clicked!');
});

// Arrow function version
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    console.log('Button clicked!');
});

// Access event details
button.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    console.log('Clicked at:', event.clientX, event.clientY);
    event.target.style.backgroundColor = 'blue';
});

// Common events
// - click: Mouse click
// - dblclick: Double click
// - mouseenter: Mouse enters element
// - mouseleave: Mouse leaves element
// - keydown: Key pressed
// - keyup: Key released
// - submit: Form submitted
// - input: Input value changed
// - change: Input value changed and lost focus
// - scroll: Page or element scrolled
// - load: Page finished loading

// Form submission example
const form = document.querySelector('form');

form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();  // Prevent page reload

    const formData = new FormData(form);
    const name = formData.get('name');
    console.log('Name submitted:', name);
});
```

### JavaScript Best Practices

1. ✅ **Use `const` by default**, `let` when needed, avoid `var`
2. ✅ **Use meaningful variable names** - `userName` not `x`
3. ✅ **Use strict equality** - `===` instead of `==`
4. ✅ **Comment complex logic** - Explain the "why", not the "what"
5. ✅ **Keep functions small** - One function, one purpose
6. ✅ **Use arrow functions** - More concise and cleaner
7. ✅ **Handle errors** - Use try/catch for operations that might fail
8. ✅ **Load scripts at end of body** - Or use `defer` attribute

